<Base title="About · Slop Canon" description="What this is, and the one rule that matters: on hard days, the colour drains out.">
  <section class="wrap" style="padding-top:44px;max-width:720px">
    <div class="band-label">
      <span class="txt">What this is</span>
      <span class="rule"></span>
    </div>
    <div class="honest" style="font-size:17px">
      <p>Every morning a machine reads a little of the day — the news, the tech, the noise of being online —
        and writes <b>one poem</b> about the feeling underneath it. Not the headline. The feeling beside the
        headline. Then a person glances at it, and it goes out, and the day is done.</p>
      <p>It's written by a language model, and that isn't hidden — it's the whole point. <b>Slop Canon</b> is
        exactly what it sounds like: a canon of slop, a project <b>about</b> the age of generated everything,
        made <b>from inside</b> it. The machine knows what it is — a hallucination trying to say something true,
        reaching for a human warmth it can watch but not quite hold.</p>
      <p><b>There is one rule that matters most.</b> On days of real, collective grief, everything changes. The
        colour drains out of the page. The jokes stop. The poem goes short and quiet, and its only job is to
        say: you are not alone in this. That restraint isn't a bug or an off-day — it's the soul of the thing.
        When in doubt about a day, the machine treats it as heavy. A quiet day costs nothing; a tasteless one
        costs everything.</p>
      <p><b>Reading the little labels.</b> Each poem carries its issue number, <b>Nº000</b> — the next in the
        series. The dashed <b>“where it came from”</b> receipt beneath it is the honesty mechanism: it says a
        machine wrote this, when, and two or three oblique notes on what it glimpsed of the day.</p>
      <p>No ads. No streaks. No infinite scroll. One poem each morning, then it leaves you alone.</p>
    </div>
    <p style="margin-top:32px"><a class="btn pink" href="/">Read today's poem →</a></p>
  </section>
</Base>
